@ilhaan - dont think thats the right approach. The name of the deployment is intentional, and maps to the api operations for openai. We can set the default value of the name property, to the same value of the model - but that would cause conflict if a user deployed multiple instances of a model.

I deffinetly see the issue - but think we need to huddle around backwards compatibility with single model deployments. When the name property is not passed, the deployment takes the name of the "<account_name>-deployment" which is not unique at scale.

```
@batchSize(1)
resource cognitiveService_deployments 'Microsoft.CognitiveServices/accounts/deployments@2023-05-01' = [
  for (deployment, index) in (deployments ?? []): {
    parent: cognitiveService
    name: deployment.?name ?? '${name}-deployments'
    properties: {
      model: deployment.model
      raiPolicyName: deployment.?raiPolicyName
    }
    sku: deployment.?sku ?? {
      name: sku
    }
  }
]
```

An account might need to have multiple deployments of a model to scale up throughput. We would just need to think through scales with different capacities. Kinda see two directions...

1. abstract the scale of models, by introducing a scale parameter that makes multiple deployments of a model (if name is provided then that is used for the "name") and the instance count is used to scale deployment names since those have to be unique. 

The below example should produce the following deployments 
`gpt-35-turbo-001, gpt-35-turbo-002, dall-e-3-001, dall-e-3-002, dall-e-3-003, dall-e-3-004
`

```
deployments: [
