### Description

I propose utilizing the inherent fine control one can have for each notification level where one can have both 'Actual' and 'Forecasted' notification in the same budget. Here is my implementation:
```bicep
@description('Notification threshold object type.')
type notificationThreshold = {
  @description('The comparison operator. The operator can be either `EqualTo`, `GreaterThan`, or `GreaterThanOrEqualTo`.')
  operator: 'EqualTo' | 'GreaterThan' | 'GreaterThanOrEqualTo'

  @description('Percent threshold of budget for when to get a notification. Must be between 1 and 1000.')
  threshold: int

  @description('The type of threshold to use for the budget. The threshold type can be either `Actual` or `Forecasted`.')
  thresholdType: 'Actual' | 'Forecasted'
}

@maxLength(5)
@description('Optional. The list of percentage thresholds at which to send notifications, together with operator and threshold type.')
param thresholds notificationThreshold[] = [
  {
    operator: 'GreaterThan'
    threshold: 50
    thresholdType: 'Actual'
  }
  {
    operator: 'GreaterThan'
    threshold: 75
    thresholdType: 'Actual'
  }
  {
    operator: 'GreaterThan'
    threshold: 90
    thresholdType: 'Actual'
  }
  {
    operator: 'GreaterThan'
    threshold: 100
    thresholdType: 'Actual'
  }
  {
    operator: 'GreaterThan'
    threshold: 110
    thresholdType: 'Actual'
  }
]

var notificationsArray = [
  for threshold in thresholds: {
    '${threshold.thresholdType}_${threshold.operator}_${threshold.threshold}_Percentage': {
      enabled: true
      operator: threshold.operator
      threshold: threshold.threshold
      contactEmails: contactEmails
      contactRoles: contactRoles
      contactGroups: actionGroups
      thresholdType: threshold.thresholdType
    }
  }
]
```

Removed parameters: thresholdType, operator

Also fixed that the name for the notification was fixed to a certain an operator and type

