Tagging @anthony-c-martin @SydneyhSmith for awareness as we were discussing the 800 limit earlier this week. 

Anything we can do to help @kmosti here

@kmosti do you care about the deployment history or do you never look at it once deployed?

Hola @kmosti,

thank you for the thorough answer. I'm currently traveling, so my first response will need to be short. However, I want to quickly explain myself a bit better regarding the 'slicing & dicing' suggestion:
You're right in that deployments to the same resource group will contribute to the same pool of 800 deployments that it can handle. However, I think we may have talked about 2 different things. If you slice the template into multiple that are still all deployed via e.g., an orchestrator template with no throttling, you'll absolutely hit the same limit again. What I am suggesting could arguably be seen as said throttling to give the backend a bit of time to clean up at the expense of the deployment time (and deleted deployment objects). This could be done in two ways:
1. You split the template into multiple files that are deployed in sequence by your orchestrating workflow. If you deploy e.g., 300 Event Hubs at a time, ARM may be fast enough to interviene before hitting the limit
2. Alternatively, an orchestrator template should work if it deploys the Namespace in one template, followed by e.g., chunks of 300 Event Hubs in another (using a loop) to which you apply a `batchSize(1)` (i.e., it's supposed to deploy one template at a time) - OR just having them all deployed in a single template but with a `batchSize(300)`.
   ```bicep
   module namespace 'br/public:avm/res/event-hub/namespace:0.15.0' = {
     params:{
       name: 'Zup?'
     }
   }

   @batchSize(300)
   module namespace_eventHubs 'br/public:avm/res/event-hub/namespace/eventhub:0.2.2' = [for (eventHub, index) in (eventHubs ?? []): {
     name: 'Bittercup-${eventHub.name}-${index}'
     params: {
       name: eventHub.name
       namespaceName: namespace.outputs.name 
     }
   }]
   ```

In either case, it's not 100% reliable, it's hard to tell where the sweetspot is and like you put it, it smells, but it may just work. Anyways, I don't want to discredit any of the points you raised and just wanted to elaborate a bit. Let's also see what other responses on this issue we may get 👍

